e is that gap between ambition and  reality and one of the realities at the  moment of course is we need to reduce  emissions by about 45 percent in the  coming decade in fact they're projected  to rise by about 14  as economies rebound from the cobit  covered pandemic so the issue we've got  that doesn't even include the conflict  in ukraine and of course that could be  galvanizing action to go for renewables  to pursue greener technologies we're  also seeing lots of economies in the u.s  and across europe scramble for fossil  fuel resources by making investments  there are we baking in commitments to  high emissions for decades to come so a  question i put to michael reagan he's  the new administrator of biden's  environmental protection agency in the  us and i ask him are we in a better  situation now than we were coming out of  the glasgow climate summit  i can say that this uh war has served as  a stark reminder  that we should not be reliant on fossil  fuels and so in the united states over  the past year 80 percent of investment  in new capacity for energy generation  has been wind solar and battery storage  if we had been making investments like  that over the past 10 to 20 years we  would not be subject to the price  volatility that we're seeing now because  of this unprovoked war so i believe it's  a reminder as harsh as it is it's a  reminder that we need to continue to  invest heavily in clean energy and wean  ourselves off of fossil fuel for the  sake of the planet for the sake of  public health but also for the sake of  national security  and what one aspect of that that your  agency i know is is particularly  involved with obviously methane  emissions from u.s um fossil fuel plants  and pipelines etc  the president has committed to a massive  reduction in methane wants the rest of  the world to join him that was announced  at the summit  are you confident you're going to be  able to maybe go to the egypt summit  with some progress to show in terms of  curbing methane emissions in the u.s  because as i understand it they're  actually increasing at the moment  i'm very confident  not only  has the president led with the global  methane pledge  which significant number of countries  have signed on to  but we've proposed the most stringent  methane regulation in united states  history and we should be finalizing that  soon  that is capturing and reducing methane  emissions from both new and existing oil  facilities so i'm very very optimistic  that we are staying the course and that  will leverage technology and leverage  new business models and leverage the  ingenuity uh of you know our private  sector to reduce methane in a way that  will provide significant benefits uh to  not only the planet but to communities  that reside near these facilities  i want to come on to that in a moment  but one question first as i understand  it the supreme court is currently  expected within days maybe  imminently to decide on whether to curb  your agency's powers  to enforce environmental regulations  particularly around emissions from  fossil fuel plants are you worried the  epa is about to be defanged by by  conservative judges  listen we're following the supreme court  cl case very closely and they will make  a decision on  a regulation that focuses directly on  uh  reducing uh emissions from power plants  uh but i've said from day one the  president has a number of tools in his  toolbox and as we look at all of the  regulations uh that govern power plants  we have regulations that look at  traditional air pollutants water  pollutants and coal ash or you know  hazardous waste cleanup we're presenting  all of those regulations at the same  time and companies will take a look at  the suite of regulations and determine  if they want to invest or double down  and invest in the past or if they want  to invest in clean energy solutions for  the future so while the supreme court  case is very important  it's not the only game in town  and before we move on just find it how  much of your  work since taking over has been to undo  a lot of the damage done to the epa by  the previous administration  listen it was uh very tough walking into  this agency and looking at  uh looking at you know old colleagues in  their eyes that i served with prior uh  during my first tour with epa and and  just seeing the damage that was done  over the past four years but you know  the the staff the career staff at epa  are resilient uh they have really hit  the ground running they're excited about  this new administration  we've proposed a number of regulations  that will curb greenhouse gas emissions  as the governor says terminate pollution  protect families protect communities and  so we feel really good we feel really  good about where the agency is today and  we're just going to keep moving forward  you're the first african american to run  the epa how much work does the u.s have  to do to address environmental racism in  your country  yes it's not lost on me that i'm the  first black man to lead this agency and  it's not lost on this administration  just how much work we have to do  to tackle environmental racism  environmental injustice but i can say  that the president has pledged that  environmental justice will be a  centerpiece of this administration  leadership matters he's given us the  ability  to pursue this issue very vigorously and  that's exactly what we're going to do  well let's get to a bit more of today's  climate news now because record floods  and rockslides have forced a rare  closure of yellowstone national park  which spans the u.s states of wyoming  idaho and montana all entries to the  park were closed for the first time  since 1988 after roads were washed away  and bridges damaged torrential rain in  one of the wettest springs in years and  a rush of water from snow melt following  a sharp rise in temperatures combined to  cause the appalling conditions  elsewhere in the u.s more than a 